      "narrative": "The door of the Copper Finch swings shut behind you, cutting off the warmth and the copper glow. The morning air hits you\u2014damp, carrying the iron tang of wet stone and the muddy smell of the street after rain.\n\nThe Ash Gate stands at the far end of the lane, a cracked black arch rising against a sky the colour of old pewter. It's not so much a gate as a wound in the air\u2014two pillars of dark stone, pitted with age, framing nothing but a view of grey mist and the suggestion of a road beyond that doesn't quite align with the ground at your feet. Beside it, the Grey Hind rots at the landing, its hull splintered, its rope frayed to dry threads.\n\nAs you approach, you notice the figure in the travel-stained cloak has reached the gate ahead of you. They stand before it, one hand resting on the cold stone, head bowed as if reading an inscription only they can see.\n\nThey haven't noticed you yet.\n\nThe mud squelches beneath your boots. The air is still. The Ash Gate looms, silent and waiting."
    },
