      "narrative": "The name echoes off the cracked stone, swallowed by the grey nothing between the pillars. For a moment, nothing. Just the drip of water from the rotting hull of the Grey Hind, the distant complaint of a gull.\n\nThen the wind shifts.\n\nIt comes through the arch, cold and smelling of ash and dust\u2014the dry scent of a place that hasn't seen rain in a hundred years. It stirses the wet grass at your feet, lifts the edge of the courier's cloak.\n\nThe courier turns.\n\nTravel-stained cloak, sharp features, eyes that have seen too many roads. They look at you with something between curiosity and wariness, their hand still resting on the cold stone of the gate.\n\n\"Old Bram's dead,\" they say. Voice low, carrying. \"Has been for a tenday. You won't get an answer from him.\"\n\nA pause. The wind dies.\n\n\"I'd know. I was here when they found him.\"\n\nThey hold your gaze, waiting to see what you make of that."
    },
