      "narrative": "You drop to a knee, running your fingers through the wet mud beneath the arch. Cold grit. A few pebbles. Nothing turns up\u2014no disturbed earth, no hidden compartment, no scrap of paper tucked beneath a stone. The ground is as solid and unremarkable as the grey sky overhead.\n\n*Fail.*\n\nThe courier watches you scrabble at the dirt with an expression that shifts from curiosity to something flatter. Disappointment? Amusement? Hard to tell.\n\n\"There's nothing hidden here,\" they say, voice dry as ash. \"You're digging at nothing.\"\n\nThey let the silence stretch, then add: \"You're not the first to come looking for secrets under this gate. Won't be the last. But the ground doesn't keep them.\"\n\nThe wind picks up again, carrying that scent of old dust and distant fire. The courier's cloak ripples. Their hand still rests on the stone. They make no move to leave\u2014but they're watching you like someone who's deciding whether you're worth more of their time."
