      "narrative": "The mud tugs at your boots as you retrace the lane, the Ash Gate shrinking behind you until it's just a dark notch in the grey. The Copper Finch's copper lamps glow through the wet air ahead, a warm amber promise against the morning's chill.\n\nThe door groans open. Inside, the air is thick with the smell of old ale, damp wool, and woodsmoke. A few patrons nurse morning cups in the dim light\u2014a pair of dockworkers arguing in low voices near the hearth, a lone merchant picking at a plate of bread and cheese.\n\nYour table stands in the corner by the shuttered window. The same one you sat at before. The wood is scarred with old rings from countless cups, the bench creaks as you settle onto it.\n\nMara Venn is gone. Her cup still sits on the bar, half-full, abandoned.\n\nA serving girl\u2014young, with a smudge of flour on her cheek\u2014notices you and wanders over.\n\n\"Something warm?\" she asks. \"Ale? Stew's not ready yet, but there's bread and salted pork.\"\n\nThe fire crackles. Rain taps at the shutters."
